UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 27, Lesson 3: Modality and balanced language', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Balanced language uses careful modality (might, may, suggests) when evidence is partial and avoids absolute always/never without proof.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSoften: \"This proves every family must agree.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nThis suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that.\n\nCHECK\nStrong modality on weak evidence misleads readers.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRewrite eight absolute claims into balanced modality. Label certainty and justify two choices.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Balanced language uses careful modality (might, may, suggests) when evidence is partial and avoids absolute always/never without proof.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-VOCAB-01","EN2-SPELL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Rewrite eight absolute claims into balanced modality. Label certainty and justify two choices. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using modality on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers.. Correction: This suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating modality on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with modality, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about modality with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for modality. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for modality. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["This suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that","Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Soften: \"This proves every family must agree.\" Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for modality; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["This suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that","Careful use of balanced and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers","Balanced language uses careful modality (might, may, suggests) when evidence is partial and avoids absolute"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with modality?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named balanced and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name balanced and explain why it matters only, without linking it to modality","Apply modality to new material and name the balanced and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers"],"question":"Which response best applies modality with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries modality. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show modality and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers"],"question":"Read: \"At dawn, Mina whispered to her goat as frost glittered on the fence.\" Which choice best uses modality from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name balanced and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","This suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that","Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about modality?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse balanced and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for modality?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about modality","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of modality on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about modality with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating modality on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using modality on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for modality. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with modality, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers.. Correction: This suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w27-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise modality.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use modality with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify balanced and explain why it matters.","I can practise might independently on a new prompt.","I can notice suggest needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Balanced language uses careful modality (might, may, suggests) when evidence is partial and avoids absolute always/never without proof.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","Balanced language uses careful modality (might, may, suggests) when evidence is partial and avoids absolute"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about modality with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"The survey suggests some students want longer recess\" is balanced; \"Everyone hates short recess forever\" is not.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSoften: \"This proves every family must agree.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nThis suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that.","explain":"Balanced language uses careful modality (might, may, suggests) when evidence is partial and avoids absolute always/never without proof.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Modality for balanced discussion language.","caption":"Match certainty to evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Balanced</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">suggests some…</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Absolute</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">everyone forever</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"The survey suggests some students want longer recess\" is balanced; \"Everyone hates short recess forever\" is not. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"The survey suggests some students want longer recess\" is balanced; \"Everyone hates short recess forever\" is not. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about modality with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"The survey suggests some students want longer recess\" is balanced; \"Everyone hates short recess forever\" is not. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Softened claims contrasted with overclaim.","caption":"Justify two choices.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Soften</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">must → may</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">strong words, weak proof</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about modality with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStrong modality on weak evidence misleads readers. Here is a more productive model: \"The survey suggests some students want longer recess\" is balanced; \"Everyone hates short recess forever\" is not.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThis suggests some families may agree — if the evidence shows that.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Strong modality on weak evidence misleads readers. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about modality with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBalanced language uses careful modality (might, may, suggests) when evidence is partial and avoids absolute always/never without proof. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 27, Lesson 3: Modality and balanced language","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Discussion texts and comparing viewpoints","week":27,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"The survey suggests some students want longer recess\" is balanced; \"Everyone hates short recess forever\" is not.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Modality for balanced discussion language.","caption":"Match certainty to evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Balanced</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">suggests some…</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Absolute</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">everyone forever</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Softened claims contrasted with overclaim.","caption":"Justify two choices.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Soften</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">must → may</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">strong words, weak proof</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y4-w27-l3' AND family_id IS NULL;
